 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "keritot.11a.10",
   "daf": "11a",
   "sentenceId": 9,
   "he": "אֵיזוֹהִי שִׁפְחָה כּוּ׳. תָּנוּ רַבָּנַן: ״וְהׇפְדֵּה״, יָכוֹל כּוּלָּהּ? תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר: ״לֹא נִפְדָּתָה״. יָכוֹל לֹא נִפְדָּתָה? תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר: ״וְהׇפְדֵּה״,",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «Which is» the espoused «maidservant?» According to Rabbi Yehuda, it is any woman who is half-maidservant and half-free woman, whereas Rabbi Yishmael maintains that it is referring to a full-fledged maidservant. In relation to this dispute, «the Sages taught» in a baraita that deals with the verse: «“And she was redeemed”» (Leviticus 19:20): One «might» have thought that this means she was «entirely» redeemed. Therefore, «the verse states:» “And she was redeemed «and not redeemed,”» to teach that she was not entirely redeemed. If so, one «might» have thought that she «was not redeemed» at all. Therefore, «the verse states: “And she was redeemed,”» to teach that it is discussing a maidservant who was partially redeemed."
  }
 ],
